3. How Approach A Works in React 

The frontend does not have to hardcode account names. It can dynamically discover the account keys using Object.keys() and create chart series from them. 

const accountNames = Object.keys(response.data[0]).filter(key => key !== "date"); 

{accountNames.map(name => ( 

<Line 

   key={name} 

   dataKey={name} 

   name={`${name} (${response.type[name]})`} 

/> 

))} 

  4. Why We Are NOT Selecting Approach A 

Repeated account-name keys: Every date row repeats the same account names as JSON property names. With many dates, this increases the uncompressed payload. 

Dynamic property names are less structured: The JSON schema changes when a business/display name changes. The account name is simultaneously being used as data and as a property identifier. 

Display name becomes part of the data structure: A display label such as 'Saving Bank A/c' is better treated as metadata rather than as a structural JSON key. 

Type metadata is separate: The type map must be maintained separately and looked up using the same dynamic name. 

Scaling becomes less efficient: As the number of dates and account categories grows, the same property-name strings are repeated in every row. 

Chart-friendly, but not domain-oriented: The response is convenient for Recharts because dataKey can directly point to a property, but the API shape is influenced by how the chart consumes data. 

Important: Approach A is not technically wrong. Object.keys() makes it possible to build the chart without hardcoding account names. The decision against A is primarily about reducing repeated data, creating a more explicit API contract, and scaling the time-series representation. 

 

 

5. Approach B — Shared Dates + Series Values 

Approach B stores the common date axis once. Each account/category is represented as one series, and its values are stored in the same order as the shared dates. 





