6. Why B Shared Dates + Series Values Is Selected 

Dates are sent only once: All account series use the same date axis, so the API does not repeat date strings for every account. 

Account names are sent only once: Each account name appears once inside its series metadata rather than once for every date. 

Values are compact: The time-series measurements are represented as numeric arrays, which avoids repeating date/name/type fields for every point. 

Natural time-series model: The response explicitly represents one common X-axis and multiple Y-series, matching the chart concept. 

Better scalability: As the number of dates and series grows, the response avoids a large amount of repeated structural information. 

Clear separation of metadata and measurements: Series metadata such as name and type is separate from the numeric values. 

Chart-library independence: The API describes the data rather than returning chart-specific x/y objects. A frontend adapter can map it to Recharts or another library. 

Small frontend transformation: The frontend only needs to associate dates[i] with values[i]. This is predictable and easy to test.


  

7. Frontend Processing for Approach B 

The frontend reads the series dynamically. No account names are hardcoded. Each value is paired with the date at the same array index. 

  

const chartSeries = response.series.map(series => ({ 

name: series.name, 

type: series.type, 

data: response.dates.map((date, index) => ({ 

   x: date, 

   y: series.values[index] 

})) 

})); 

  

For Saving Bank A/c, the result becomes: 

  

[ 

{ "x": "2026-07-27", "y": 45.0 }, 

{ "x": "2026-07-28", "y": 46.5 }, 

{ "x": "2026-07-29", "y": 44.0 } 

]   

If the selected chart library can consume a shared category/X-axis plus a values array directly, the frontend can avoid creating these x/y objects and pass the arrays through an adapter. 

  

