10.Important Trade-Off B  Shared Dates +Series values is more network-efficient for a regular time series, but it introduces an index-based relationship: dates[i] corresponds to values[i]. This relationship must be validated and consistently maintained by the backend.


10.Important Trade-Off B  Shared Dates +Series values is more network-efficient for a regular time series, but it introduces an index-based relationship: dates[i] corresponds to values[i]. This relationship must be validated and consistently maintained by the backend. 

If different series can have completely different dates, or if missing dates are common and cannot be represented with null values, a per-point {date, value} structure may be safer. 

11.Final Decision Selected: B Shared Dates + Series Values 

For this requirement, B Shared Dates + Series Values is selected because it removes the two major sources of repetition: dates are transmitted once and account names are transmitted once. The frontend performs only a small deterministic mapping, while the backend remains independent of the charting library. 

Approach A remains a valid and simple option for small datasets, particularly when direct chart rendering is the highest priority. However, when the requirement explicitly includes network optimization, reduced repetition, and scalability, B + Shared Dates provides the better overall API representation. 

 
